_{3}\\) lies on at least one of the lines, and  \n* exactly two of the lines are *sunny* (i.e. their slopes are different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Sunny lines can contain at most two points of \\(T_{3}\\)\n\nA line with slope \\(m\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\) is determined by two distinct points.\nChecking all triples of points of \\(T_{3}\\) shows that none of them are collinear\nwith a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).  \nHence any sunny line meets at most two points of \\(T_{3}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  All sunny pairs\n\nCompute the slope for each unordered pair of points of \\(T_{3}\\).  \nThe pairs whose slope is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) are exactly\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &: (1,1)\\text{ and }(2,2) &&\\text{slope }1,\\\\\nB &: (1,2)\\text{ and }(3,1) &&\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12,\\\\\nC &: (1,3)\\text{ and }(2,1) &&\\text{slope }-2 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus any sunny line that contains points of \\(T_{3}\\) must be one of the three\nlines \\(\\ell_{A},\\ell_{B},\\ell_{C}\\) determined by the pairs \\(A,B,C\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Attempt to use two sunny lines\n\nSuppose we have exactly two sunny lines.  \nThey must be two of \\(\\ell_{A},\\ell_{B},\\ell_{C}\\); the third line will be non\u2011sunny.\n\n*Choose \\(\\ell_{A}\\) and \\(\\ell_{B}\\).*  \nPoints covered: \\((1,1),(2,2),(1,2),(3,1)\\).  \nRemaining points: \\((1,3)\\) and \\((2,1)\\).  \nThe line through these two points has slope \\(-2\\), which is sunny.\nHence the third line would be sunny, contradicting the requirement that only two\nlines are sunny.\n\n*Choose \\(\\ell_{A}\\) and \\(\\ell_{C}\\).*  \nRemaining points: \\((1,2)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) whose connecting line has slope\n\\(-\\tfrac12\\) (sunny). Same contradiction.\n\n*Choose \\(\\ell_{B}\\) and \\(\\ell_{C}\\).*  \nRemaining points: \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\) whose connecting line has slope \\(1\\)\n(sunny). Same contradiction.\n\nThus no selection of two sunny lines leaves a pair of points that can be joined\nby a non\u2011sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Could the non\u2011sunny line contain three points?\n\nA non\u2011sunny line may be vertical (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)), horizontal (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)),\nor have slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).  \nEach of these possibilities passes through exactly three points of \\(T_{3}\\):\n\n* \\(x=1\\) contains \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\);\n* \\(y=1\\) contains \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\);\n* \\(x+y=4\\) contains \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\).\n\nIn every case the three points left uncovered are\n\\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\), \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\), or \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) respectively.\nAny two of these remaining points are aligned with a slope in\n\\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\); consequently each of them would require a separate sunny\nline. Hence three sunny lines would be necessary, not two.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion\n\nAll possible ways to allocate the three lines either force the third line to be\nsunny or require more than two sunny lines. Therefore a configuration with\nexactly two sunny lines does **not** exist for \\(n=3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3,\\;k=2\\text{ is impossible.}}\n\\]"
