l_C\\):* the uncovered points are \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,2)\\); the line through them has slope \\(1\\) (sunny).  \n\n   In every possible selection the remaining two points determine a sunny line, so a configuration with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n5. **Could the non\u2011sunny line contain three points?**  \n   A non\u2011sunny line can be vertical (\\(x=\\text{const}\\)), horizontal (\\(y=\\text{const}\\)), or have slope \\(-1\\) (\\(x+y=\\text{const}\\)).  \n   The only such lines that pass through points of \\(T_{3}\\) are  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\qquad y=1,\\qquad x+y=4,\n   \\]\n   each containing exactly three points of \\(T_{3}\\).  \n\n   *If \\(x=1\\) is the non\u2011sunny line*, the remaining points are \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\). Any line joining two of them is either vertical, horizontal, or slope \\(-1\\) \u2013 all non\u2011sunny. Hence two sunny lines cannot cover these three points.  \n\n   *If \\(y=1\\) is the non\u2011sunny line*, the remaining points are \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\); again every pair among them yields a non\u2011sunny slope.  \n\n   *If \\(x+y=4\\) is the non\u2011sunny line*, the remaining points are \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\); the same conclusion holds.  \n\n   Thus a configuration with one non\u2011sunny line covering three points and two sunny lines covering the rest is impossible.\n\n6. **Other possibilities.**  \n   If the non\u2011sunny line covered only one or two points, the two sunny lines could cover at most four points (each sunny line \u2264\u202f2 points). Since \\(|T_{3}|=6\\), at least six points must be covered, which is impossible under those circumstances.\n\n7. **Conclusion.**  \n   All possible allocations of three lines with exactly two sunny lines lead to a contradiction. Therefore such a configuration does not exist for \\(n=3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3,\\;k=2\\text{ is impossible.}}\n\\]"
