lds the complete description of the admissible values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^2\\mid a\\ge1,\\;b\\ge1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad N_n:=|T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\).  \nThus a non\u2011sunny line is either vertical \\((x=c)\\), horizontal \\((y=c)\\) or of slope \\(-1\\) \\((x+y=c)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines\n\nFor a sunny line \\(L\\) let \\(m(L)\\) be the number of points of \\(T_n\\) that lie on \\(L\\).  \nIf the slope of \\(L\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\), the line can intersect the lattice points of the triangle at most along a line of slope \\(1\\); any other rational slope would miss at least one lattice point of each diagonal.  \nConsequently the maximal possible value of \\(m(L)\\) is attained for a line of slope \\(1\\).\n\nA line of slope \\(1\\) has equation \\(y=x-d\\) for some integer \\(d\\).  \nThe points of \\(T_n\\) on this line satisfy  \n\n\\[\na-b=d,\\qquad a+b\\le n+1,\\qquad a,b\\ge1 .\n\\]\n\nSolving gives  \n\n\\[\na\\in\\Bigl[\\max\\{1,1+d\\},\\;\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1+d}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\Bigr],\n\\]\n\nhence  \n\n\\[\nm(L)=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1-|d|}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor=:M_n .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nA non\u2011sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) (the longest diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) has exactly \\(n\\) points).\n\nAssume a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.  \nUsing (2) we obtain\n\n\\[\nN_n\\le kM_n+(n-k)n .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(N_n=\\dfrac{n(n+1)}2\\) and \\(M_n=\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le kn_{\\!s}+(n-k)n\n\\Longrightarrow\nk\\le\\frac{n(n-1)/2}{\\,n-M_n\\,}\n= \\frac{n(n-1)/2}{\\lceil (n-1)/2\\rceil } .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nEvaluating the right\u2013hand side gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn, & n\\ \\text{odd},\\\\[1mm]\nn-1, & n\\ \\text{even}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus for even \\(n\\) a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines is impossible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Constructions attaining the upper bound\n\n#### 2.1  Odd \\(n\\)\n\nWrite \\(n=2t+1\\) (\\(t\\ge1\\)).  \nFor each integer \\(d\\) with \\(|d|\\le t\\) define the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nL_d:\\;y=x-d .\n\\]\n\nThere are exactly \\(2t+1=n\\) such lines, all of slope \\(1\\) and therefore sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(|a-b|\\le t\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_{a-b}\\).  \nHence the family \\(\\{L_d\\mid |d|\\le t\\}\\) covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and uses \\(k=n\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 2.2  Even \\(n\\)\n\nWrite \\(n=2t\\) (\\(t\\ge2\\)).  \nConsider the non\u2011sunny diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD:\\;x+y=2t+1 .\n\\]\n\n\\(D\\) contains precisely the \\(n\\) points of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a+b=n+1\\).  \nThe remaining points constitute the triangle \\(T_{n-1}=T_{2t-1}\\).  \nSince \\(n-1\\) is odd, we can apply the construction of the odd case to \\(T_{n-1}\\): for each integer \\(d\\) with \\(|d|\\le t-1\\) take  \n\n\\[\nL_d:\\;y=x-d .\n\\]\n\nThese are \\(n-1\\) distinct sunny lines, and every point of \\(T_{n-1}\\) satisfies \\(|a-b|\\le t-1\\), hence lies on the appropriate \\(L_d\\).  \nTogether the \\(n-1\\) sunny lines \\(\\{L_d\\}\\) and the single non\u2011sunny line \\(D\\) form a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all of \\(T_n\\) with exactly \\(k=n-1\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Obtaining every smaller value of \\(k\\)\n\nLet \\(k_{\\max}\\) be the maximal admissible number given by (4).  \nStart from the configuration described in \u00a72 that uses \\(k_{\\max}\\) sunny lines.\n\n*If \\(k<k_{\\max}\\):*  \nReplace any \\(k_{\\max}-k\\) of the sunny lines by distinct non\u2011sunny diagonals \\(x+y=c\\) (with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)).  \nEach point of \\(T_n\\) lies on exactly one diagonal, so after the replacement every point that lost its sunny line is still covered by the newly inserted diagonal.  \nBecause there are \\(n-1\\) different diagonals and we need at most \\(k_{\\max}-k\\le n-1\\) of them, this replacement is always possible.  \n\n*If \\(k=0\\):*  \nWhen \\(n\\) is odd we replace all \\(n\\) sunny lines by the \\(n-1\\) diagonals \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,n+1\\) and add a vertical line \\(x=1\\) (or a horizontal line) to reach a total of \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines.  \nWhen \\(n\\) is even we keep the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) from the even\u2011\\(n\\) construction, replace the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines by the other \\(n-2\\) diagonals, and again add a vertical line to obtain \\(n\\) non\u2011sunny lines.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (4) a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\)\n\nAssume \\(n=2t\\) and that a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines exists.  \nFrom (2) we have \\(M_n=\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor = t\\).  \nUsing the inequality derived in \u00a71 with \\(k=n\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n\\cdot t = n\\frac{n}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}}2 ,\n\\]\n\nwhich simplifies to \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le\\frac{n^{2}}2\\), i.e. \\(n+1\\le n\\), a contradiction.  \nHence no such configuration exists; consequently for even \\(n\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is excluded.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nCombining the constructions of \u00a72\u2013\u00a73 with the impossibility proved in \u00a74 we obtain the complete description:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is odd: } &&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\},\\\\\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is even: }&&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,