nct non‑sunny diagonals \\(x+y=c\\) (with \\(2\\le c\\le n+1\\)).  \nEach point of \\(T_n\\) lies on exactly one diagonal, so after the replacement every point that lost its sunny line is still covered by the newly inserted diagonal.  \nBecause there are \\(n-1\\) different diagonals and we need at most \\(k_{\\max}-k\\le n-1\\) of them, this replacement is always possible.  \n\n*If \\(k=0\\):*  \nWhen \\(n\\) is odd we replace all \\(n\\) sunny lines by the \\(n-1\\) diagonals \\(x+y=2,\\dots ,n+1\\) and add a vertical line \\(x=1\\) (or a horizontal line) to reach a total of \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines.  \nWhen \\(n\\) is even we keep the diagonal \\(x+y=n+1\\) from the even‑\\(n\\) construction, replace the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines by the other \\(n-2\\) diagonals, and again add a vertical line to obtain \\(n\\) non‑sunny lines.\n\nThus for every integer \\(k\\) satisfying (4) a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Impossibility of \\(k=n\\) for even \\(n\\)\n\nAssume \\(n=2t\\) and that a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines exists.  \nFrom (2) we have \\(M_n=\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\rfloor = t\\).  \nUsing the inequality derived in §1 with \\(k=n\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le n\\cdot t = n\\frac{n}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}}2 ,\n\\]\n\nwhich simplifies to \\(\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\le\\frac{n^{2}}2\\), i.e. \\(n+1\\le n\\), a contradiction.  \nHence no such configuration exists; consequently for even \\(n\\) the value \\(k=n\\) is excluded.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 5.  Conclusion\n\nCombining the constructions of §2–§3 with the impossibility proved in §4 we obtain the complete description:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is odd: } &&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\},\\\\\n&\\text{If }n\\text{ is even: }&&k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\end{aligned}}\n\\]\n\nThese are exactly the non‑negative integers for w