y lines and one non‑sunny line exist.  \n   *Case analysis* on the possible non‑sunny line (vertical, horizontal, or slope \\(-1\\)) shows that after removing the points covered by that line, the remaining three points cannot be covered by two sunny lines because any two of them are either vertical, horizontal, or have slope \\(-1\\) (all non‑sunny). Hence a configuration with \\(k=2\\) is impossible.\n\n6. **\\(k=3\\).**  \n   All three lines must be sunny.  \n   The only sunny collinear pairs among the six points are  \n\n   \\[\n   (1,1)-(2,2),\\quad (1,2)-(3,1),\\quad (1,3)-(2,1),\n   \\]\n\n   with slopes \\(1,\\,-\\tfrac12,\\,-2\\) respectively.  \n   Taking the three lines determined by these pairs gives\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_{1}&:y=x,\\\\\n   L_{2}&:y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52,\\\\\n   L_{3}&:y=-2x+5,\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   which are distinct, all sunny, and together cover every point of \\(T_{3}\\). Thus \\(k=3\\) is attainable.\n\nSince every possible \\(k\\) has been examined and the conclusions are rigorous, the complete set of admissible values for \\(n=3\\) is \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\). ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
