cal L_{n-1}\\) of \\(n-1\\) distinct sunny lines covers \\(T_{n-1}\\).  \n  Add the point \\((n,1)\\) (the only point of \\(T_n\\) with \\(a=n\\)).  \n  Choose a slope \\(\\sigma\\) satisfying  \n\n  \\[\n  \\sigma\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\cup\\{\\text{slope of any line in }\\mathcal L_{n-1}\\},\n  \\]\n\n  which is possible because the forbidden set is finite.  \n  Define  \n\n  \\[\n  S_n:\\; y-1=\\sigma\\,(x-n).\n  \\]\n\n  Then \\(S_n\\) is sunny, distinct from all lines of \\(\\mathcal L_{n-1}\\), and passes through \\((n,1)\\).  \n  The union \\(\\mathcal L_n=\\mathcal L_{n-1}\\cup\\{S_n\\}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines and covers \\(T_n\\) (the old points are covered by \\(\\mathcal L_{n-1}\\), the new point by \\(S_n\\)).  \n  This completes the induction.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Construction for every \\(k\\ge 3\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) and put \\(m=n-k\\ge 0\\).\n\n* Use the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  V_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,m).\n  \\]\n\n  They are non\u2011sunny and cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate satisfies \\(a\\le m\\).\n\n* The uncovered points are those with \\(a\\ge m+1\\).  \n  Writing \\(a'=a-m\\) and \\(b'=b\\), the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes  \n\n  \\[\n  a'+b'\\le (n-m)+1 = k+1,\n  \\]\n\n  i.e. the set of uncovered points is exactly \\(T_k\\) (translated by the vector \\((m,0)\\)).\n\n* By the result of Section\u202f1, \\(T_k\\) can be covered by \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines.  \n  Translating those lines by \\((m,0)\\) yields \\(k\\) sunny lines covering the uncovered points of the original problem.\n\nThus we obtain a family of \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny vertical lines together with \\(k\\) sunny lines, altogether \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that for some \\(n\\ge3\\) there exists a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines with exactly two sunny lines satisfying the covering condition.\n\nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(n-1,1),\\qquad P_2=(n-1,2),\\qquad P_3=(n,1),\n\\]\n\nall belonging to \\(T_n\\) because \\((n-1)+2=n+1\\) and \\(n+1\\le n+1\\).\n\nAny line through two of these points is:\n\n* \\(P_1P_2\\): the vertical line \\(x=n-1\\) (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis);\n* \\(P_1P_3\\): the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis);\n* \\(P_2P_3\\): the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nAll three directions are **non\u2011sunny** by definition. Consequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the points \\(\\{P_1,P_2,P_3\\}\\).\n\nWith only two sunny lines we can cover at most two of the three points, leaving at least one point uncovered \u2013 contradicting the hypothesis that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on some line.  \nHence no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Collection of all admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFrom Sections\u202f1\u20133 we have:\n\n* Existence for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\);\n* Existence for every \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) (by the construction of Section\u202f2);\n* Non\u2011existence for \\(k=2\\).\n\nTherefore, for any integer \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nare precisely the non\u2011negative integers for which a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two stated conditions exists. \u220e"

