            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem requires that every point of \\(T_n\\) lie on at least one of the \\(n\\) chosen lines.  \nA line is **sunny** iff its slope \\(\\sigma\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Constructions for the extreme values  \n\n##### 2.1  \\(k=0\\) (no sunny line)  \n\nTake the \\(n\\) distinct vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach line is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); therefore the point satisfies the equation of \\(L_a\\).  \nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of the chosen lines and exactly \\(0\\) of them are sunny.  \n\n*Verification*:  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\). Since \\(a\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\), the line \\(x=a\\) (i.e. \\(L_a\\)) contains \\((a,b)\\). ∎\n\n---\n\n##### 2.2  \\(k=1\\) (exactly one sunny line)  \n\nReplace the vertical line \\(x=n\\) by the line  \n\n\\[\nS:\\;y=x-(n-1).\n\\]\n\n\\(S\\) has slope \\(1\\), which is sunny.  \nAll other \\(n-1\\) lines are the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).  \n\n*Verification*:  \n\n* If \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\le n-1\\), then \\(x=a\\) and the point lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \n* If \\(a=n\\), the only possible point is \\((n,1)\\) (since \\(n+1\\le n+1\\)). Substituting into \\(S\\): \\(1 = n-(n-1)\\) holds, so \\((n,1)\\) lies on the sunny line.  \n\nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered and exactly one line is sunny. ∎\n\n---\n\n##### 2.3  All‑sunny case \\(k=n\\)  \n\nWe prove by induction that for every \\(n\\ge3\\) there exist \\(n\\) **distinct** sunny lines covering \\(T_n\\).\n\n---\n\n**Base \\(n=3\\).**  \n\nThe six points of \\(T_3\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,1),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\nConsider the three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y = x,               &&\\text{slope }1,\\\\\nL_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52, &&\\text{slope }-\\tfrac12,\\\\\nL_3 &: y = -2x +5,          &&\\text{slope }-2.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll slopes avoid \\(\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\); the lines are distinct.  \nWe verify point‑by‑point:\n\n| Point \\((a,b)\\) | Lies on which line? | Check |\n|----------------|----------------------|-------|\n| (1,1) | \\(L_1\\) | \\(1 = 1\\) ✓ |\n| (2,2) | \\(L_1\\) | \\(2 = 2\\) ✓ |\n| (1,2) | \\(L_2\\) | \\(2 = -\\tfrac12\\cdot1 + \\tfrac52 = 2\\) ✓ |\n| (3,1) | \\(L_2\\) | \\(1 = -\\tfrac12\\cdot3 + \\tfrac52 = 1\\) ✓ |\n| (1,3) | \\(L_3\\) | \\(3 = -2\\cdot1 +5 = 3\\) ✓ |\n| (2,1) | \\(L_3\\) | \\(1 = -2\\cdot2 +5 = 1\\) ✓ |\n\nThus the three sunny lines cover all points of \\(T_3\\). ∎\n\n---\n\n**Inductive step.**  \nAssume for some \\(n\\ge4\\) that there exists a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{n-1}=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_{n-1}\\}\n\\]\n\nof \\(n-1\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_{\\,n-1}\\).  \nThe set \\(T_n\\) contains exactly one point not in \\(T_{\\,n-1}\\), namely  \n\n\\[\nP_n=(n,1).\n\\]\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{\\text{slope of }L_i\\mid i=1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\cup\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n\\(S\\) is a finite subset of \\(\\mathbb R\\). Choose a real number \\(\\sigma\\notin S\\) (possible because \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus S\\neq\\varnothing\\)).  \nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\nL_n:\\; y-1=\\sigma\\,(x-n)\\qquad\\text{(i.e. }y=\\sigma x+1-\\sigma n\\text{)}.\n\\]\n\n* \\(L_n\\) passes through \\(P_n\\) by construction.  \n* Its slope \\(\\sigma\\) is not \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and differs from the slopes of all lines in \\(\\mathcal L_{n-1}\\); therefore \\(L_n\\) is sunny and distinct from each \\(L_i\\).  \n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_n=\\mathcal L_{n-1}\\cup\\{L_n\\}.\n\\]\n\nEvery point of \\(T_{\\,n-1}\\) lies on some line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n-1}\\) (induction hypothesis), and \\(P_n\\) lies on \\(L_n\\).  \nHence \\(\\mathcal L_n\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThus, by induction, for every \\(n\\ge3\\) there exists a covering of \\(T_n\\) by exactly \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines. ∎\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  Construction for every \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) satisfy \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nm=n-k\\ge0 .\n\\]\n\n**Step 1 – non‑sunny vertical lines.**  \nTake the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,m).\n\\]\n\nThese are distinct, non‑sunny, and cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate \\(a\\) satisfies \\(a\\le m\\).\n\n*Verification*:  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and \\(a\\le m\\), then \\(x=a\\) is exactly one of the lines \\(V_i\\); hence the point lies on a chosen line.\n\n**Step 2 – description of the remaining points.**  \nFor a point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a>m\\) write  \n\n\\[\na=m+a',\\qquad a'>0 .\n\\]\n\nThe condition \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) becomes  \n\n\\[\n(m+a')+b\\le n+1\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;a'+b\\le (n-m)+1 = k+1 .\n\\]\n\nThus the set of uncovered points is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\{(m+a',b)\\mid a',b>0,\\;a'+b\\le k+1\\}= (m,0)+T_k .\n\\]\n\n**Step 3 – sunny covering of the translate.**  \nFrom Section 2.3 we have \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines \\(\\widetilde L_1,\\dots ,\\widetilde L_k\\) that cover every point of \\(T_k\\).  \nTranslate each of them by the vector \\((m,0)\\):\n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;(x,y)\\mapsto (x-m,y)\\in\\widetilde L_i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k).\n\\]\n\nTranslation does not change the slope, so each \\(L_i\\) remains sunny and the \\(L_i\\) stay distinct.\n\n*Verification*:  \nTake any uncovered point \\((a,b)=(m+a',b)\\) with \\((a',b)\\in T_k\\).  \nSince \\((a',b)\\) lies on some \\(\\widetilde L_{i}\\), we have \\((a',b) = (x',y')\\) with \\((x',y')\\in\\widetilde L_i\\).  \nAdding \\((m,0)\\) gives \\((a,b)=(x'+m,y')\\in L_i\\).  \nHence every point of the translate \\((m,0)+T_k\\) lies on one of the lines \\(L_i\\).\n\n**Step 4 – final family.**  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_m\\}\\cup\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(m+k=n\\) distinct lines, precisely \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal F\\). ∎\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that for some \\(n\\ge3\\) there exists a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly two sunny lines.\n\nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(n-1,1),\\qquad P_2=(n-1,2),\\qquad P_3=(n,1).\n\\]\n\nAll three satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), hence belong to \\(T_n\\).\n\nAny line through two of these points is:\n\n* \\(P_1P_2\\): the vertical line \\(x=n-1\\) (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis);\n* \\(P_1P_3\\): the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis);\n* \\(P_2P_3\\): the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nAll three directions are **non‑sunny** by definition.  \nConsequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the points \\(\\{P_1,P_2,P_3\\}\\).\n\nWith only two sunny lines we can cover at most two of these three points, leaving at least one uncovered, contradicting the requirement that every point of \\(T_n\\) be covered.\n\nTherefore no configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists for any \\(n\\ge3\\). ∎\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Collection of all admissible \\(k\\)\n\nFrom the constructions we have exhibited configurations for  \n\n* \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\);\n* every integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).\n\nFrom the impossibility argument we have shown that \\(k=2\\) cannot occur.\n\nHence, for any integer \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nare precisely the non‑negative integers for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem. ∎\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
