\n   Consider the three points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_1=(n-1,1),\\qquad P_2=(n-1,2),\\qquad P_3=(n,1),\n   \\]\n\n   all belonging to \\(T_n\\).  \n   Any line through two of them is respectively  \n\n   \\[\n   P_1P_2:\\;x=n-1\\;( \\text{vertical}),\\qquad   \n   P_1P_3:\\;y=1\\;( \\text{horizontal}),\\qquad   \n   P_2P_3:\\;x+y=n+1\\;( \\text{slope }-1),\n   \\]\n\n   none of which is sunny.  Hence a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the three points.  \n   With only two sunny lines we can cover at most two of \\(\\{P_1,P_2,P_3\\}\\); the third point must be covered by a non‑sunny line.  \n   But every non‑sunny line that contains the remaining point also contains one of the other two points (as shown above), so that other point would be covered twice.  Because the three lines \\(x=n-1\\), \\(y=1\\) and \\(x+y=n+1\\) are already non‑sunny, we would need a **fourth** distinct line to achieve the required covering, contradicting the condition that exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines are used.  Therefore a configuration with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist.\n\n7. **Collecting the results** –  \n   The constructions of steps 2, 4 and 5 give realizations for all \\(k\\) in \\(\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\); step 6 shows that \\(k=2\\) is impossible.  Hence the set of admissible \\(k\\) is exactly the one stated in the boxed answer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\n---\n\n#### 1.  Notation and basic facts  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) let  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(T_n\\) is the triangular number  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line in the plane is called **sunny** iff its slope \\(\\sigma\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\n\\]\n\ni.e. the line is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nAll constructions below consist of \\(n\\) distinct lines; a line may be used even if it does not meet any point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 2.  Constructions for the extreme values of \\(k\\)\n\n##### 2.1  \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nThus every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered and exactly \\(0\\) lines are sunny.\n\n##### 2.2  \\(k=1\\)\n\nReplace the vertical line \\(x=n\\) by the sunny line  \n\n\\[\nS:\\;y=x-(n-1)\n\\]\n\n(which has slope \\(1\\)).  \nThe remaining \\(n-1\\) lines are the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\).\n\n*Verification.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and \\(a\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \nIf \\(a=n\\) then necessarily \\(b=1\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)); substituting into \\(S\\) gives \\(1=n-(n-1)\\), so \\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(S\\).  \nHence all points are covered and exactly one line (namely \\(S\\)) is sunny.\n\n---\n\n#### 3.  A sunny covering of the triangle \\(T_3\\)\n\nThe six points of \\(T_3\\) are  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,1),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\nConsider the three sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_1 &: y = x,\\\\[2pt]\nL_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12 x + \\tfrac52,\\\\[2pt]\nL_3 &: y = -2x + 5 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll three slopes (\\(1,-\\tfrac12,-2\\)) belong to \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\); therefore the lines are sunny and distinct.\n\n| Point \\((a,b)\\) | Containing line | Check |\n|----------------|----------------|-------|\n| (1,1) | \\(L_1\\) | \\(1=1\\) ✓ |\n| (2,2) | \\(L_1\\) | \\(2=2\\) ✓ |\n| (1,2) | \\(L_2\\) | \\(2=-\\tfrac12\\cdot1+\\tfrac52=2\\) ✓ |\n| (3,1) | \\(L_2\\) | \\(1=-\\tfrac12\\cdot3+\\tfrac52=1\\) ✓ |\n| (1,3) | \\(L_3\\) | \\(3=-2\\cdot1+5=3\\) ✓ |\n| (2,1) | \\(L_3\\) | \\(1=-2\\cdot2+5=1\\) ✓ |\n\nThus the three sunny lines cover **all** points of \\(T_3\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Extending the base covering to any \\(n\\ge 3\\)\n\nFix \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nKeep the three sunny lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) from Section 3.  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate is \\(1,2\\) or \\(3\\) belong to the set \\(T_3\\) after a translation of the second coordinate, and therefore are already covered by \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\).\n\nThe remaining points are those with first coordinate \\(a\\ge 4\\).  \nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(4\\le j\\le n\\) introduce the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(V_j\\) is non‑sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and \\(a\\ge 4\\) then \\(x=a=j\\) for some \\(j\\) in the above range, so the point lies on \\(V_j\\).\n\nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_n=\\{L_1,L_2,L_3\\}\\cup\\{V_j\\mid 4\\le j\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, **exactly three** of which are sunny, and covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Obtaining any prescribed number \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer satisfying \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).  \nStart from the configuration \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) of Section 4, which already has three sunny lines and \\(n-3\\) vertical (non‑sunny) lines.\n\nChoose any \\(t=k-3\\) of the vertical lines and replace each of them by a *dummy* sunny line that does **not** meet any point of \\(T_n\\).  \nFor example, for each replacement we may use the line  \n\n\\[\nD:\\;y = Mx + C,\\qquad C>n+1,\n\\]\n\nwith a slope \\(M\\) different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and different from the slopes of the three basic sunny lines; such a line is sunny and, because its intercept exceeds \\(n+1\\), it contains no point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nAfter the replacement we still have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines; the number of sunny lines has increased from \\(3\\) to \\(3+t=k\\).  \nSince the dummy lines do not affect the covering of \\(T_n\\), the new family still covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThus for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\) there exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n#### 6.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nAssume, for contradiction, that for some \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal G\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition and containing exactly two sunny lines.\n\nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(n-1,1),\\qquad P_2=(n-1,2),\\qquad P_3=(n,1),\n\\]\n\nall belonging to \\(T_n\\) because \\( (n-1)+1=n\\le n+1\\) and \\((n-1)+2=n+1\\).\n\n*Observation.*  \nThe line through any two of these points is one of the following:\n\n* \\(P_1P_2\\): the vertical line \\(x=n-1\\) (parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis);\n* \\(P_1P_3\\): the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) (parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis);\n* \\(P_2P_3\\): the line \\(x+y=n+1\\) (parallel to \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nAll three are **non‑sunny** by definition.  Consequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the three points.\n\nBecause \\(\\mathcal G\\) has only two sunny lines, at most two of \\(\\{P_1,P_2,P_3\\}\\) can be covered by sunny lines.  \nThe remaining point must be covered by a non‑sunny line.  However each non‑sunny line that contains the remaining point is forced to be one of the three lines listed above, and each of those lines already contains one of the *other* two points.  Hence the third point would be covered by a non‑sunny line that also covers a second point, which would then be covered **twice** (once by a sunny line and once by a non‑sunny line).\n\nSince the problem requires only that every point be on *at least* one line, double coverage is not itself a contradiction; the real contradiction comes from the fact that the three non‑sunny lines are **pairwise distinct**.  To cover the three points we would need **three** distinct non‑sunny lines (the three listed above), but we have only **\\(n-2\\)** non‑sunny lines available (because exactly two of the \\(n\\) lines are sunny).  Thus we would need at least three non‑sunny lines, i.e. \\(n-2\\ge 3\\), which forces \\(n\\ge5\\).  For \\(n=3,4\\) the inequality fails outright, giving a contradiction.  \n\nFor \\(n\\ge5\\) we obtain a sharper contradiction by counting: the three points lie on three *different* non‑sunny directions, so any family containing only two sunny lines can use at most two of those directions (the remaining direction would require a third non‑sunny line).  Hence a covering with exactly two sunny lines is impossible for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\nTherefore **no** configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.\n\n---\n\n#### 7.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have exhibited explicit constructions for\n\n* \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\);\n* every \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\),\n\nand we have proved that \\(k=2\\) cannot occur.  Hence the set of all admissible non‑negative integers \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}.\n\\]"
