e sunny lines cover **all** points of \\(T_3\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 4.  Extending the base covering to any \\(n\\ge 3\\)\n\nFix \\(n\\ge 3\\).  \nKeep the three sunny lines \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\) from Section 3.  \nAll points of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate is \\(1,2\\) or \\(3\\) belong to the set \\(T_3\\) after a translation of the second coordinate, and therefore are already covered by \\(L_1,L_2,L_3\\).\n\nThe remaining points are those with first coordinate \\(a\\ge 4\\).  \nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(4\\le j\\le n\\) introduce the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(V_j\\) is non‑sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and \\(a\\ge 4\\) then \\(x=a=j\\) for some \\(j\\) in the above range, so the point lies on \\(V_j\\).\n\nConsequently the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_n=\\{L_1,L_2,L_3\\}\\cup\\{V_j\\mid 4\\le j\\le n\\}\n\\]\n\ncontains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, **exactly three** of which are sunny, and covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n---\n\n#### 5.  Obtaining any prescribed number \\(k\\) with \\(3\\le k\\le n\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer satisfying \\(3\\le k\\le n\\).  \nStart from the configuration \\(\\mathcal F_n\\) of Section 4, which already has three sunny lines and \\(n-3\\) vertical (non‑sunny) lines.\n\nChoose any \\(t=k-3\\) of the vertical lines and replace each of them by a *dummy* sunny line that does **not** meet any point of \\(T_n\\).  \nFor example, for each replacement we may use the line  \n\n\\[\nD:\\;y = Mx + C,\\qquad C>n+1,\n\\]\n\nwith a slope \\(M\\) different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) and different from the slopes of the three basic sunny lines; such a line is sunny and, because its intercept exceeds \\(n+1\\), it contains no point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nAfter the replacement we still have exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines; the number of sunny lines has increased from \\(3\\) to \\(3+t=k\\).  \nSince the dummy lines do not affect the covering of \\(T_n\\), the new family still covers every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\nThus