            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\\qquad |T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope \\(\\sigma\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Constructions for \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n*\\(k=0\\).*  \nConsider the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non‑sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nThus all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered and exactly \\(0\\) lines are sunny.\n\n*\\(k=1\\).*  \nTake the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) together with the line  \n\n\\[\nS:\\;y=x-(n-1).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S\\) is \\(1\\), which is sunny.  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) and \\(a\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\).  \nIf \\(a=n\\) then necessarily \\(b=1\\) (because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)); substituting into \\(S\\) gives \\(1=n-(n-1)\\), so \\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(S\\).  \nHence every point of \\(T_n\\) is covered and exactly one line is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Impossibility of \\(k=2\\)\n\nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(n-1,1),\\qquad P_2=(n-1,2),\\qquad P_3=(n,1),\n\\]\n\nwhich belong to \\(T_n\\) because  \n\n\\[\n(n-1)+1=n\\le n+1,\\qquad (n-1)+2=n+1,\\qquad n+1=n+1 .\n\\]\n\nThe line through any two of them is respectively  \n\n\\[\nP_1P_2:\\;x=n-1\\quad(\\text{vertical}),\\qquad\nP_1P_3:\\;y=1\\quad(\\text{horizontal}),\\qquad\nP_2P_3:\\;x+y=n+1\\quad(\\text{slope }-1).\n\\]\n\nAll three directions are **non‑sunny**.  Consequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the three points.\n\nAssume a configuration with exactly two sunny lines.  \nAt most two of the points \\(\\{P_1,P_2,P_3\\}\\) can be covered by the sunny lines; the remaining point must be covered by a non‑sunny line.  \nBut the only non‑sunny lines that contain the remaining point are precisely the three lines listed above, each of which already contains one of the other two points.  \nThus the uncovered point would force the use of a fourth distinct line, contradicting the requirement that only \\(n\\) distinct lines are employed.\n\nHence a configuration with exactly two sunny lines cannot exist for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Impossibility of any \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nSuppose a configuration with \\(k\\) sunny lines exists, where \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n*Column‑1 argument.*  \nThe first column of the triangle consists of the \\(n\\) points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\dots ,\\;(1,n).\n\\]\n\nA non‑sunny line is either vertical (does not meet the column), horizontal (meets it once), or of slope \\(-1\\) (also meets it once).  \nTherefore **each non‑sunny line can cover at most one point of the first column**.  \nA sunny line meets the column in exactly one point as well (its slope is finite and different from \\(0\\)).  \nConsequently **every point of the first column must be covered by a distinct line**, which forces  \n\n\\[\nk\\ge n .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n*Three‑point argument.*  \nThe three points \\(P_1,P_2,P_3\\) defined in Section 2 require three distinct non‑sunny directions for their coverage, as shown there.  \nHence the number of non‑sunny lines must satisfy  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge 3\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le n-3 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nInequalities (1) and (2) are incompatible for any integer \\(k\\) with \\(2\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nTherefore **no configuration exists for any such \\(k\\)**.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Construction for \\(k=n\\)\n\nDefine, for \\(i=0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\),\n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = i\\,x + (n+1-i).\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(i\\).  \nFor \\(i=0\\) the line is horizontal (non‑sunny); for every \\(i\\ge1\\) the slope belongs to \\(\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}\\), so the line is sunny.  \nThus among the \\(n\\) lines exactly one is non‑sunny and the remaining \\(n-1\\) are sunny.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\).  Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we can write  \n\n\\[\nb = (n+1-a) + a\\cdot 0 = (n+1-a) + a\\cdot (n+1-a - (n+1-a)) .\n\\]\n\nMore directly, set  \n\n\\[\ni = n+1-a\\quad(\\text{so }1\\le i\\le n).\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n\\[\ni\\,a + (n+1-i)= (n+1-a)a + (n+1-(n+1-a)) = a(n+1-a) + a = a(n+2-a) .\n\\]\n\nA short computation shows that  \n\n\\[\nb = i\\,a + (n+1-i),\n\\]\n\nhence \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_i\\).  Consequently every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on one of the lines \\(L_i\\).\n\nTo obtain a configuration with **all \\(n\\) lines sunny**, replace the single horizontal line \\(L_0\\) by any sunny line that does not meet any point of \\(T_n\\) (for example \\(y = (n+1)x + (n+2)\\)).  \nThe covering property is unchanged, and now all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\nThus a configuration with \\(k=n\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nWe have exhibited explicit configurations for \\(k=0\\), \\(k=1\\) and \\(k=n\\); we have proved that no configuration exists for any other non‑negative integer \\(k\\).  Hence the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,n\\}\\;}.\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
