n,1)\\) lies on \\(S\\).  \n   Thus every point of \\(T_n\\) belongs to at least one of the chosen lines and exactly one line (the line \\(S\\)) is sunny.\n\n5. **Impossibility of any \\(k\\ge 2\\).**  \n   Inequality (1) already shows that no configuration can contain two or more sunny lines. Hence for every \\(n\\ge3\\) the only possible values of \\(k\\) are \\(0\\) and \\(1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope \\(\\sigma\\) satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\sigma\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nC_1=(1,1),\\qquad C_2=(1,n),\\qquad C_3=(n,1),\n\\]\n\nwhich all belong to \\(T_n\\) because \\(1+1\\le n+1\\), \\(1+n=n+1\\) and \\(n+1=n+1\\).\n\n*If a line contains two of the points \\(C_1,C_2,C_3\\) then its direction is respectively*\n\n*   \\(C_1C_2:\\) horizontal \\(\\;(y\\text{-axis parallel})\\);\n*   \\(C_1C_3:\\) vertical \\(\\;(x\\text{-axis parallel})\\);\n*   \\(C_2C_3:\\) slope \\(-1\\) (parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\nAll three directions are **non\u2011sunny**.  \nHence a sunny line can contain **at most one** of the three points \\(C_1,C_2,C_3\\).\n\nSince the three points are distinct, any family of \\(n\\) lines that covers all points of \\(T_n\\) must contain at least three distinct lines to cover \\(C_1,C_2,C_3\\).  \nAmong those three lines at most one can be sunny, therefore the total number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le 1}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Existence of a configuration with \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) is parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence non\u2011sunny.  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nThus all points of \\(T_n\\) are covered and exactly \\(k=0\\) lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Existence of a configuration with \\(k=1\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-1)\n\\]\n\ntogether with the line  \n\n\\[\nS:\\;y=x-(n-1).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S\\) equals \\(1\\); therefore \\(S\\) is sunny, while each \\(V_i\\) is non\u2011sunny.\n\n*Verification that every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of these lines.*\n\n*   If \\(a\\le n-1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the vertical line \\(V_a\\).\n*   If \\(a=n\\) the inequality \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) forces \\(b=1\\).  \n    Substituting \\((n,1)\\) into (4) gives \\(1=n-(n-1)\\), so \\((n,1)\\) lies on \\(S\\).\n\nHence the set \\(\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-1},S\\}\\) covers all points of \\(T_n\\) and contains exactly one sunny line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Non\u2011existence of configurations with \\(k\\ge 2\\)\n\nInequality (3) proved in Section\u202f1 shows that any admissible family of \\(n\\) lines can contain **at most one** sunny line.  \nTherefore no configuration with \\(k\\ge 2\\) exists for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 5.  Conclusion  \n\nThe only non\u2011negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1\\}} .\n\\]"
